\euclide creates a Euclidean rhythm, spreading a number of
onsets over the taps of a bar as evenly as possible.
It must be placed inside the notes definition of an
instrument, inside a bar definition.
Its syntax is the following:

\euclide {taps onsets [duration [offset]] [notes] [dynamics]}

The bar is divided into "taps" equal steps (e.g. 16 taps in
4/4 are 16ths), and "onsets" is the number of notes spread
over them. Without a duration, an onset lasts one tap, and
rests fill the space until the next onset:

\bar euc-1 {
    \time 4/4
    \inst1 \euclide {16 5 c''}
}

The duration (which can be dotted, e.g. 4.) is used if it
fits before the next onset, otherwise the longest duration
that fits. The offset is a number of onset durations of rest
at the start of the bar. If the onsets don't fit after it,
the offset is made smaller, with a warning:

    \inst1 \euclide {16 5 4 1 c' cis' <c' ees'>}

The notes are applied to the onsets in turn. Without notes,
the onsets are durations only, for rhythmic instruments.
Dynamics among the notes are applied in turn too, and are
written only when they change:

    \inst1 \euclide {16 5 4 c' cis' \f \p}

Errors worth knowing: taps and onsets must be integers
greater than 0, the onsets can't be more than the taps, and
the bar must be divisible into the taps (taps can't be more
than the 64ths of the bar).
